    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Chinese Brand AITO Plans 2027 European Launch\nHyundai Recalls Genesis G80 Sedans Over Potential Seat Fires\n10 photos\nPhoto: Genesis\nFitted to a plethora of Hyundai Genesis and Genesis G80 models, a plastic switch fitted to non-memory power seats can either come loose or become misaligned after taking a hit. In either case, it can stay engaged, increasing the risk of a fire because the seat motor overheats due to prolonged operation.\nProlonged operation is possible with park selected or while driving. The NHTSA filing indicates that Hyundai sold 94,622 potentially affected sedans in the US market. Hyundai further highlights that the switch can hold the circuit closed with the car shut off, which makes the safety risk all the more serious.\nThere is no fix yet, with the remedy being under development as we speak. The interim measure is to park outside, away from structures and other vehicles, until the repair is approved by the safety boffins at Hyundai Motor America. The affected population's 17-character VINs became searchable on October 13, 2026, yet dealers will get their instructions on December 6, 2026.\nSomewhat unsurprisingly, owners will get their notification letters in the first week of December as well. The recall population splits between 54,644 examples of the 2015 through 2016 model year Hyundai Genesis , built from February 2014 through February 2016, and 39,978 copies of the 2017 through 2020 model year Genesis G80, built from March 2016 through November 2019.\nAlthough the badge changed for 2017, the car underneath is pretty much the same. Both the Hyundai and Genesis models were assembled exclusively in South Korea. Hyundai estimates that only 1 percent of the recall population carries the defect, which means fewer than 1,000 cars in the US market.\nPhoto: Genesis The warnings associated with the recall condition are pretty thin, with Hyundai Motor America noting a control switch that stops responding and a burning odor from the seat. Similar to certain battery recalls, this one also comes with a park outside advisory for affected owners. The switch assemblies were supplied by Nidec Mobility Korea, with the recall documentation showing two part numbers associated with the driver-side and passenger-side switches.\nHyundai Motor America became aware of this problem on New Year's Eve 2021, prompted by a 2018 model year G80 whose subject parts could not be received. A second case was reported in August 2022, and this time, the subject components confirmed external impact as the cause behind the damaged switch. The following years passed with field monitoring and low incident rates.\nA review from August 2025 found no additional fire claims in the US market. Everything was peaceful until July 2026, when Korean-market thermal event allegations forced Hyundai Motor America to reopen its investigation. The recall decision ultimately landed at the beginning of October 2025, just under five years after the first report.\nRegardless of when the fix becomes available, dealers are required to remedy subject vehicles free of charge. Owners who already paid someone to chase this smell or replace defective switches can claim reimbursement under the plan Hyundai Motor America filed with the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nGenesis G80 Hyundai Genesis Genesis Hyundai recall\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT New Hot Wheels RLC Exclusive Model Is an RWB Porsche That You Probably Can’t Buy",
